The goal of person re-identification is to accurately identify individuals across different camera systems that may have disparate perspectives and varied data sources. A significant issue in re-ID systems is attribute mismatch, which arises when subjects with the same attributes are incorrectly matched due to differences in attribute descriptions. To address this problem, we introduce a novel approach that aligns attribute descriptions at the cluster level, enabling more precise and consistent attribute representations. This approach involves two main components: attribute alignment at the person level and cluster-based aggregation of attributes. We utilize a similarity matrix to measure attribute alignment and propose a probabilistic aggregation model to determine cluster membership. A thorough experimental evaluation on four standard re-ID datasets demonstrates the effectiveness of our approach, outperforming current state-of-the-art methods in attribute alignment. Furthermore, our approach's ability to incorporate attribute information leads to improved re-ID performance, with the potential for further performance gains through the combination with other re-ID methods. The source code for this project is available on GitHub at https://github.com/ChenHua1995/Cluster-level-Attribute-Alignment-for-ReID. \author{Hao Chen, Sheng-Cheng Liu, Wei Dong, Dong-Ke Feng, Shouyi Zhang, and
